<!--
  "What your browser reveals" box.
  Everything is computed by assets/js/fingerprint.js inside the visitor's browser.
  Nothing is stored or sent anywhere: the script makes no network requests.
-->
<section class="fp-box" id="fingerprint" aria-labelledby="fp-title">
  <h2 id="fp-title">what your browser just told me</h2>
  <p class="fp-intro">
    Any website can read a lot of small details about your device without asking. One by one they are boring. Put together they make a
    <em>browser fingerprint</em>, which is often enough to recognise you on a later visit, even after you clear your cookies. This is yours.
  </p>

  <button type="button" class="btn fp-button" id="fp-reveal">
    <i class="fa-solid fa-fingerprint" aria-hidden="true"></i> Show me what my browser shares
  </button>

  <div class="fp-results" id="fp-results" hidden>
    <dl class="fp-grid" id="fp-grid"></dl>

    <div class="fp-id">
      <div class="fp-id-label">Your fingerprint</div>
      <div class="fp-id-value" id="fp-id"></div>
      <p class="fp-id-note">
        A short code built from the details above. Studies of real browsers have found that combinations like this are unique for many
        visitors, and that is why fingerprinting gets used for tracking. Browsers such as Tor Browser and Brave push back by making their users
        look alike.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="fp-privacy">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-lock" aria-hidden="true"></i>
      All of this runs in your browser. Nothing is stored or sent anywhere, and it is gone when you leave the page.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<script src="{{ '/assets/js/fingerprint.js' | relative_url | bust_file_cache }}" defer></script>
